Alright, I've just confirmed that the goal refinement mechanism is working: the TUI now automatically sets a goal based on the model's response. However, I also noticed that the model still prefixes its output with the literal string “GOAL: ” (or variations like “Goal: ”). This prefix is being carried through the pipeline and ends up in the UI, which is undesirable—users shouldn't see that internal marker.

Where does this refined goal text get processed? Scanning the source, I recall that after the model response is received, the TUI code trims the content and stores it in a variable called `refined`. The relevant snippet is in `src/tui.tsx`:

```tsx
.then((res) => {
  const refined = res.content.trim();
  // …
```

This is exactly where the raw model output is turned into the refined goal string that the UI displays and uses for further actions. To strip the unwanted prefix, I need to apply a regex replace after trimming. The pattern should match “goal” optionally followed by a colon, case‑insensitively, and any surrounding whitespace at the start of the string. A suitable replace call is:

```tsx
.trim().replace(/^goal:?\s*/i, "")
```

That will remove “goal”, an optional colon, and any following spaces, regardless of case. I don't need to touch any other parts of the code because this is the sole place where the refined string is initially derived; downstream code already works with `refined`.

Thus, the minimal change is to replace the line initializing `refined` with the version that includes the replace call. I'll make a targeted edit in `src/tui.tsx`, replacing the old line with the new one. This should clean up the UI output and eliminate the stray “GOAL:” prefix.Edit input={"replace_all": false, "file_path": "/home/lane/GR/glint-cli/src/tui.tsx", "old_string": "          .then((res) => {\n            const refined = res.content.trim();", "new_string": "          .then((res) => {\n            const refined = res.content.trim().replace(/^goal:?\\s*/i, \"\");"}
